### Render verification method (explicit)
I used a **real headless browser that was already installed** — Playwright's cached `chrome-headless-shell` (`~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-1234/…`). Nothing was installed. Two passes:
1. `chrome-headless-shell --dump-dom --virtual-time-budget=6000 http://localhost:3000/` → post-JS DOM showed `<section class="card" data-state="ready">` and `<p id="message" class="message">hello world</p>`.
2. A throwaway Node script driving the **Chrome DevTools Protocol** over Node 22's built-in `WebSocket` to capture computed styles, console/exception/network events, and a screenshot (`/tmp/hw-screenshot.png`, since deleted from the profile dirs). The screenshot shows a glass card on an aurora-gradient background with a blue-gradient "hello world" wordmark — i.e. styling is genuinely applied, not just linked.

(Note: the system `Google Chrome.app` binary hung in `--headless` mode; the Playwright headless shell worked. Both temp profiles were removed.)

### Fixes applied
**None.** Every contract hook, route, status code, content type and DOM/CSS selector already lined up. I edited no repo files.

### Remaining risks (non-blocking, no change made)
- `src/server.js:55` — a request containing an encoded slash (`/..%2f..%2fsrc%2fdb.js`) makes `readFile` throw `ERR_INVALID_FILE_URL_PATH`, which is caught and logged with a full stack before returning the correct `404 {"error":"not found"}`. **No file leak**, but a remote caller can spam the log with stack traces. Optional hardening: in `handleStatic`, reject `pathname` containing `%2f`/`%5c` (or treat non-`ENOENT` as 404 without logging) before resolving the URL.
- `README.md` "Verify" block backgrounds the server with `npm start &` and never stops it; accurate but leaves a stray process for a human. Cosmetic only — deliberately left unchanged per the surgical-fix rule.
- No favicon is shipped and none is referenced, so browsers will issue one 404 for `/favicon.ico`. Harmless.


